/** One paragraph per free agency summer, written from that summer's moves. */
export const offseasonOverviews: Record<number, string> = {
  2004: 'The summer the Lakers chose Kobe Bryant over Shaquille O\'Neal. Miami got O\'Neal without giving up an All-Star and was instantly called a power in a weak East; the doubters asked whether a 32-year-old center coming off a career-low season could still carry a team. He could, for two years: a 59-win season and, with Dwyane Wade, the 2006 title.',
  2007: 'Boston turned a 24-win team into a contender in a month, adding Ray Allen and then Kevin Garnett for a package of young players and picks. The cost in luxury tax was the worry. The result was 66 wins and the 2008 title, while Minnesota started over.',
  2010: 'The summer of The Decision. LeBron James picked Miami over Cleveland, Chicago and New York and joined Dwyane Wade and Chris Bosh. The backlash was fierce and the expectations absurd, from 70 wins to "not two, not three." Miami won two titles in four Finals; Cleveland won 19 games.',
  2012: 'Days before the season, Oklahoma City traded James Harden, its 23-year-old sixth man, to Houston rather than pay him. Most graders liked it for both sides and expected the Thunder to keep contending with Kevin Durant and Russell Westbrook. Harden became an MVP; the Thunder never made it back to the Finals with that core.',
  2013: 'Draft night belonged to Brooklyn, which bought Paul Pierce and Kevin Garnett from Boston with three first-round picks and was called an instant contender. The picks were expected to land in the 20s. Instead the Nets collapsed and the picks became Jaylen Brown and Jayson Tatum.',
  2014: 'LeBron James went home. Almost nobody thought Cleveland was possible after how he left, and Miami had always seemed the favorite to keep him. The warning was years of growing pains; instead the Cavaliers reached four straight Finals and won in 2016.',
  2016: 'Kevin Durant joined the 73-win Warriors weeks after they beat his Thunder, and the word dynasty was out before he played a game. It held: titles in 2017 and 2018. Oklahoma City kept Russell Westbrook, who won MVP.',
  2017: 'Kyrie Irving asked out of Cleveland and was traded to Boston for Isaiah Thomas and the Brooklyn pick, a deal graded as a heist for the Cavaliers. Neither side got what it wanted: Cleveland lost LeBron James a year later, and Irving left Boston after two seasons.',
  2018: 'A summer of stars on the move. Dallas traded up on draft night for Luka Doncic, LeBron James signed with the Lakers in what was called the quietest bombshell in free agency history, and Toronto gambled DeMar DeRozan on a year of Kawhi Leonard. The gamble paid off with the 2019 title.',
  2019: 'The wildest summer of the era. The Lakers traded their young core for Anthony Davis, and Kawhi Leonard, a month after winning a title, chose the Clippers, who paid Shai Gilgeous-Alexander and a pile of picks for Paul George. The Clippers were named title favorites. The Lakers won the 2020 title instead, and Gilgeous-Alexander became an MVP and champion in Oklahoma City.',
};
